---
# The probes' POSITIVE CONTROL, and the reason it is a policy rather than an accident.
#
# Every probe job needs one leg that MUST succeed. Without it, an inverted leg ("this connection
# must be refused") passes for the wrong reason whenever the probe pod has no network, DNS is
# down, or the target is simply dead. Slices 1 and 2 got that leg from `homepage:3000`, which
# worked only because homepage happened to be unfenced.
#
# Slice 4.5 labels homepage, and slice 5 sets `podSelector: {}` — after which NO pod in this
# namespace is unfenced and the incidental control cannot be recreated. So the control target is
# named here instead. This is also STRICTLY STRONGER than the leg it replaces: reaching an
# unfenced pod proved only that the probe had a network, where reaching a FENCED pod that admits
# us proves the admit path itself still works. That distinguishes "denied by policy" — what the
# inverted legs assert — from "the policy engine is dropping everything".
#
# speedtest, deliberately, and NOT littlelink: littlelink is the slice-1 probe's DENIED target
# (netpol-probe-job.yaml.j2 assertion 4), so admitting the probes there would make that probe
# report POLICY NOT ENFORCED. speedtest is a static bandwidth page, is no probe's target, and has
# no data and no dependents.
#
# Adding a probe? Give its pod `netpol-probe: control` and dial `speedtest:80`.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: netpol-sentinel
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: speedtest
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
{# Renders allow-all when the flag is false, so `netpol_baseline_enforced` is a real rollback
   lever. `| bool` for the string trap. Both explained in full in
   networkpolicy-traefik.yaml.j2. #}
{% if netpol_baseline_enforced | bool %}
    # speedtest's real callers (traefik, prometheus) come from the baseline policy, which already
    # selects it. This rule adds the probe pods and nothing else. Port 80 is the containerPort,
    # which for speedtest equals the Service port — verified 2026-08-20.
    - from:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              netpol-probe: control
      ports:
        - port: 80
          protocol: TCP
{% else %}
    # OFF. One empty rule admits everything. `ingress: []` would be the exact opposite and deny
    # everything, which is the easiest thing in this file to get backwards.
    - {}
{% endif %}
